WEBVTT
NOTE what-is-carbon-fiber-made-of - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.298
Polyacrylonitrile enters an oxygen-free
furnace at around 1500 degrees Celsius and

00:00:06.298 --> 00:00:11.675
emerges as carbon fiber containing over
ninety percent carbon by mass.

00:00:11.675 --> 00:00:16.502
That figure comes from ASTM
International's standard test methods for

00:00:16.502 --> 00:00:18.251
carbon fiber composition.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:21.471
The transformation is not a coating or an
infusion.

00:00:21.471 --> 00:00:26.586
It is the controlled removal of hydrogen,
nitrogen and oxygen until only a carbon

00:00:26.586 --> 00:00:31.448
skeleton remains, and the precision
required to keep that skeleton aligned is

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:33.595
what makes the material expensive.

00:00:33.595 --> 00:00:35.520
What is carbon fiber made of?

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.800
Polyacrylonitrile, abbreviated PAN, is a
synthetic polymer.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.588
Each repeat unit contains a
carbon-nitrogen triple bond projecting

00:00:44.588 --> 00:00:45.893
from the backbone.

00:00:45.893 --> 00:00:49.329
That bond is the reason PAN works as a
precursor.

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:54.517
When heated in air, those triple bonds
react with oxygen and link adjacent

00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:58.443
polymer chains together without breaking
the main chain.

00:00:58.443 --> 00:01:03.446
The result is a stabilized structure that
will not melt or drip when you remove the

00:01:03.446 --> 00:01:05.917
oxygen and raise the temperature further.

00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:11.865
About ninety percent of commercial carbon
fiber starts as PAN, according to ASTM

00:01:11.865 --> 00:01:14.541
International's published standards.

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:18.691
The remaining ten percent comes from
petroleum pitch or rayon, but those routes

00:01:18.691 --> 00:01:21.789
produce different fiber grades and are not
interchangeable.

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:27.175
PAN was first synthesized in the 1930s,
but its use as a carbon fiber precursor

00:01:27.175 --> 00:01:32.288
was not developed until the 1960s when
researchers at multiple institutions

00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:37.128
discovered that controlled oxidation could
stabilize the polymer before

00:01:37.128 --> 00:01:38.083
carbonization.

00:01:38.083 --> 00:01:44.239
The PAN fiber enters an oxidizing oven at
temperatures between 200 and 300 degrees

00:01:44.239 --> 00:01:44.840
Celsius.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.469
Oxygen diffuses into the fiber and
triggers a cascade of reactions.

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:55.134
The nitrile groups on adjacent chains link
up, forming ladder-like structures that

00:01:55.134 --> 00:01:56.931
lock the polymer in place.

00:01:56.931 --> 00:02:02.316
The process takes one to two hours and
turns the white PAN fiber dark brown or

00:02:02.316 --> 00:02:02.731
black.

00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:07.629
If you skip this step and go straight to
high heat, the polymer melts and the fiber

00:02:07.629 --> 00:02:08.219
collapses.

00:02:08.219 --> 00:02:13.091
The stabilization step is slow because the
reactions release heat, and if the fiber

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:16.320
heats too quickly it degrades instead of
cross-linking.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.785
Manufacturers control the oven atmosphere,
tension on the fiber and heating rate to

00:02:21.785 --> 00:02:23.365
keep the process stable.

00:02:23.365 --> 00:02:27.343
The tension is critical because it
prevents the fiber from shrinking

00:02:27.343 --> 00:02:30.912
unevenly, which would create weak spots in
the final product.

00:02:30.912 --> 00:02:36.714
The stabilized fiber moves into an inert
atmosphere furnace at temperatures between

00:02:36.714 --> 00:02:38.811
1000 and 1500 degrees Celsius.

00:02:38.811 --> 00:02:41.616
Nitrogen gas or argon prevents oxidation.

00:02:41.616 --> 00:02:46.610
At these temperatures, hydrogen and
nitrogen atoms leave the structure as

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:47.979
small gas molecules.

00:02:47.979 --> 00:02:52.310
Oxygen, already reduced during
stabilization, continues to depart.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:57.167
What remains is a structure where carbon
atoms have rearranged into sheets

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.480
resembling graphite.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.837
The sheets align roughly parallel to the
fiber axis because the original polymer

00:03:03.837 --> 00:03:06.315
backbone constrained their formation.

00:03:06.315 --> 00:03:11.215
The fiber shrinks as atoms leave, and
tension applied during carbonization helps

00:03:11.215 --> 00:03:12.379
maintain alignment.

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:17.070
The higher the carbonization temperature,
the more complete the conversion and the

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.901
higher the final carbon content.

00:03:18.901 --> 00:03:24.491
A fiber carbonized at 1000 degrees Celsius
might contain ninety-three percent carbon,

00:03:24.491 --> 00:03:29.883
while one treated at 1500 degrees Celsius
can reach ninety-five percent or higher.

00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:35.287
Some applications require graphitization,
a further heat treatment at temperatures

00:03:35.287 --> 00:03:37.792
between 2000 and 3000 degrees Celsius.

00:03:37.792 --> 00:03:42.011
At this stage, the graphite-like sheets
grow larger and more ordered.

00:03:42.011 --> 00:03:46.780
Carbon content rises above ninety-five
percent, sometimes reaching ninety-nine

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:47.269
percent.

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:52.160
The fiber becomes stiffer and more
thermally conductive, but also more

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.831
Graphitization is expensive because of the
energy required and the specialized

00:03:56.831 --> 00:04:01.022
furnaces that can sustain those
temperatures without contaminating the

00:04:01.022 --> 00:04:01.381
fiber.

00:04:01.381 --> 00:04:06.317
The furnaces must use graphite heating
elements and inert atmospheres maintained

00:04:06.317 --> 00:04:07.243
at high purity.

00:04:07.243 --> 00:04:10.564
Aerospace applications sometimes justify
the cost.

00:04:10.564 --> 00:04:15.746
Most structural carbon fiber stops at
carbonization because the property gains

00:04:15.746 --> 00:04:19.931
from graphitization do not offset the
expense for typical uses.

00:04:19.931 --> 00:04:25.013
A single carbon fiber filament is about
seven micrometers in diameter, according

00:04:25.013 --> 00:04:27.936
to ASTM International's measurement
standards.

00:04:27.936 --> 00:04:32.842
That dimension is determined when the PAN
fiber is extruded, before any heat

00:04:32.842 --> 00:04:33.488
treatment.

00:04:33.488 --> 00:04:37.531
The fiber shrinks slightly during
stabilization and carbonization, but the

00:04:37.531 --> 00:04:40.427
diameter remains in the five to ten
micrometer range.

00:04:40.427 --> 00:04:45.600
Thousands of filaments are bundled into a
tow, and tows are woven or laid up into

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.963
the shapes you see in finished parts.

00:04:47.963 --> 00:04:53.349
The small diameter gives the fiber a high
surface area relative to its volume, which

00:04:53.349 --> 00:04:56.619
matters for resin adhesion in composite
structures.

00:04:56.619 --> 00:05:01.240
A typical tow might contain three
thousand, twelve thousand or twenty-four

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.987
thousand individual filaments, depending
on the application.

00:05:04.987 --> 00:05:09.707
Carbon fiber straight from carbonization
has a smooth, inert surface.

00:05:09.707 --> 00:05:12.853
Epoxy and other resins do not bond well to
it.

00:05:12.853 --> 00:05:17.540
Manufacturers treat the surface by passing
the fiber through an electrolytic bath or

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:18.768
exposing it to plasma.

00:05:18.768 --> 00:05:23.487
The treatment etches the surface and adds
oxygen-containing functional groups.

00:05:23.487 --> 00:05:28.267
The roughness and the chemical groups
together allow resin to wet the fiber and

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:30.445
form a mechanical and chemical bond.

00:05:30.445 --> 00:05:35.109
Without surface treatment, a composite
part can delaminate under load because the

00:05:35.109 --> 00:05:37.181
fiber simply pulls out of the resin.

00:05:37.181 --> 00:05:40.809
The treatment is a standard step in
commercial production.

00:05:40.809 --> 00:05:45.812
Some manufacturers also apply a sizing, a
thin polymer coating that protects the

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:49.752
fiber during handling and further improves
resin compatibility.

00:05:49.752 --> 00:05:53.948
I find a clean audit trail beautiful. A
file named correctly.

00:05:53.948 --> 00:05:56.563
A number you can follow to its source.

00:05:56.563 --> 00:06:00.287
A system whose failures leave evidence
instead of excuses.

00:06:00.287 --> 00:06:04.397
Most people see administrative debris; I
see order made visible.

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:09.574
The carbon atoms in the fiber are arranged
in sheets that run parallel to the fiber

00:06:09.574 --> 00:06:09.885
axis.

00:06:09.885 --> 00:06:13.800
When you pull on the fiber, you are
pulling against carbon-carbon bonds within

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.963
those sheets, which are among the
strongest bonds in chemistry.

00:06:16.963 --> 00:06:21.589
Tensile strength along the fiber axis can
exceed three gigapascals for

00:06:21.589 --> 00:06:26.547
high-performance grades, according to data
published by ASTM International.

00:06:26.547 --> 00:06:31.188
Strength perpendicular to the fiber axis
is much lower because you are relying on

00:06:31.188 --> 00:06:34.627
weaker forces between the sheets, not the
bonds within them.

00:06:34.627 --> 00:06:38.596
Compressive strength is also lower because
the sheets can buckle.

00:06:38.596 --> 00:06:43.053
This anisotropy means carbon fiber
composites must be designed with fiber

00:06:43.053 --> 00:06:44.275
orientation in mind.

00:06:44.275 --> 00:06:49.812
A part loaded in tension along the fiber
direction performs very differently from

00:06:49.812 --> 00:06:52.205
one loaded in compression or shear.

00:06:52.205 --> 00:06:57.217
Producing one kilogram of carbon fiber
requires about 286 megajoules of energy,

00:06:57.217 --> 00:07:01.341
according to estimates published in
materials science literature.

00:07:01.341 --> 00:07:06.159
That is higher than aluminum at roughly
170 megajoules per kilogram, and much

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:10.915
higher than steel at around 35 megajoules
per kilogram for recycled content.

00:07:10.915 --> 00:07:15.746
The energy goes into heating the fiber
through multiple stages and maintaining

00:07:15.746 --> 00:07:17.171
controlled atmospheres.

00:07:17.171 --> 00:07:22.823
The PAN precursor itself is also expensive
because it must be high purity and the

00:07:22.823 --> 00:07:25.475
polymerization process is specialized.

00:07:25.475 --> 00:07:30.479
These costs are why carbon fiber remains a
premium material used where its

00:07:30.479 --> 00:07:33.523
strength-to-weight ratio justifies the
price.

00:07:33.523 --> 00:07:38.326
The aerospace and automotive industries
are the largest consumers, followed by

00:07:38.326 --> 00:07:40.728
sporting goods and wind turbine blades.

00:07:40.728 --> 00:07:45.398
Carbon fiber composites are difficult to
recycle because the fiber is locked in a

00:07:45.398 --> 00:07:46.493
cured resin matrix.

00:07:46.493 --> 00:07:51.441
Pyrolysis, heating the composite in an
oxygen-free environment, burns off the

00:07:51.441 --> 00:07:53.304
resin and recovers the fiber.

00:07:53.304 --> 00:07:57.859
The recovered fiber is shorter because it
fractures during handling, and it has lost

00:07:57.859 --> 00:08:01.384
the alignment and surface treatment that
gave it full properties.

00:08:01.384 --> 00:08:06.450
Mechanical properties of parts made from
recycled fiber are lower than those made

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:07.576
from virgin fiber.

00:08:07.576 --> 00:08:12.906
Research continues on methods to preserve
fiber length and alignment, but as of now,

00:08:12.906 --> 00:08:17.538
most carbon fiber composite waste is
landfilled or incinerated for energy

00:08:17.538 --> 00:08:18.109
recovery.

00:08:18.109 --> 00:08:22.818
Some companies are exploring chemical
recycling methods that dissolve the resin

00:08:22.818 --> 00:08:27.587
without damaging the fiber, but these
processes are not yet economical at scale.

00:08:27.587 --> 00:08:31.629
The link between composition and
properties is direct.

00:08:31.629 --> 00:08:37.841
ASTM International publishes test methods
that measure carbon content by combustion

00:08:37.841 --> 00:08:38.515
analysis.

00:08:38.515 --> 00:08:43.334
The same standards specify carbonization
temperatures and measure tensile strength

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:44.685
on the resulting fiber.

00:08:44.685 --> 00:08:49.289
When a manufacturer reports a fiber with
ninety-five percent carbon content and a

00:08:49.289 --> 00:08:54.120
tensile strength of four gigapascals, both
numbers trace to these standardized tests.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:58.997
The process control required to hit those
numbers consistently is part of why carbon

00:08:58.997 --> 00:09:03.875
fiber production remains concentrated in a
few companies with decades of experience.

00:09:03.875 --> 00:09:09.593
Small variations in temperature, tension
or atmosphere during any stage can produce

00:09:09.593 --> 00:09:12.349
fiber that fails to meet specifications.

00:09:12.349 --> 00:09:16.841
The energy intensity figure for carbon
fiber production varies widely in

00:09:16.841 --> 00:09:22.019
published literature, from around 200 to
over 400 megajoules per kilogram depending

00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:26.947
on the production route, the efficiency of
the equipment and the energy source.

00:09:26.947 --> 00:09:31.291
The 286 megajoules per kilogram cited here
is a mid-range estimate.

00:09:31.291 --> 00:09:35.829
Precise figures are often proprietary
because they reveal details of a

00:09:35.829 --> 00:09:37.320
manufacturer's process.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.544
The comparison to aluminum and steel is
useful for understanding relative

00:09:41.544 --> 00:09:45.709
magnitude, but the absolute numbers should
be understood as approximate.

00:09:45.709 --> 00:09:48.840
Carbon fiber is not a coating on something
else.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.928
It is the product of removing everything
from a polymer except the carbon, and

00:09:53.928 --> 00:09:56.733
arranging what remains into aligned
sheets.

00:09:56.733 --> 00:10:01.338
The strength comes from the arrangement,
and the cost comes from the precision

00:10:01.338 --> 00:10:02.637
required to create it.
